{{- template "require-profile" . -}}
{{- $caps := index .profiles .profile "capabilities" -}}
{{- /* Codex parity of the Claude hook registrations (#181, the Codex side of
       #137; #199 for the quality loop). Same capabilities as the Claude side:
       enableGitHubIsolatedReader registers the safe-gh read-steering PreToolUse
       hook (GitHub injection guard, epic #119) so raw `gh` reads of untrusted
       GitHub content are nudged toward the safe-gh isolated reader, and
       enableQualityLoopHooks registers the quality-loop pair (agent-tools#203):
       PostToolUse / Edit|Write -> personal-fast-edit-check (steering) and Stop
       -> personal-changed-scope-qa (best-effort gate, blocks once per new dirty
       scope; Codex ignores a Stop matcher, so none is emitted). Both quality
       hooks are silent no-ops until a repo is declared in the user-owned
       ~/.config/agent-tools/checks.local.json. Codex-specific honest-label for
       fast-edit-check: the documented apply_patch payload carries the patch in
       tool_input.command and no file_path, so on Codex it degrades to a silent
       no-op unless a future payload adds one (measured at #199 deploy). Second
       honest-label for changed-scope-qa: its BLOCK path (exit 2 + stderr) is
       schema-independent, but its non-blocking warn path (exit 0 + stdout
       hookSpecificOutput{hookEventName:"Stop", additionalContext}) is the
       Claude Code shape; whether Codex accepts it for Stop is measured at
       #199 deploy (Codex review on PR #200) — worst case the warning is
       dropped as invalid hook output, never a block (fail-open).

       Why a SEPARATE file and not ~/.codex/config.toml's [hooks] table:
       config.toml is a codex-owned live file (codex writes [hooks.state] etc.),
       so templating it risks an overwrite conflict. The user-layer hooks.json is
       an equivalent registration point (agent-tools probe, Codex 0.142.2) and
       keeps dotfiles' managed surface disjoint from codex's live state
       (registration = dotfiles / config.toml = codex; docs/config-ownership.md).

       registration = dotfiles, body = agent-tools: the hook body
       bodies (personal-safe-gh-hook, personal-fast-edit-check, personal-
       changed-scope-qa) are deployed by agent-tools to a stable path
       (agent-tools#146 contract); dotfiles only references them by absolute
       path — no script body is distributed from here. The schema under "hooks"
       is identical to Claude Code's (event -> matcher -> command hooks); the
       whole "hooks" object is shared with the Claude registration via
       .chezmoitemplates/agent-hooks-json (#137/#181/#199 parity — edit it once
       there).

       Rendered JSON is the MINIMAL {"hooks": {...}} — no top-level "description"
       or any other key. Codex 0.142.5 rejects unknown top-level keys in
       hooks.json (a top-level "description" is a parse error in the TUI hook-
       review path, so the hook fails to load), even though the 0.142.2 plugin
       example carried one. So all human-facing notes live in THIS template
       comment, never in the rendered JSON (#185). test-codex-settings.sh pins the
       top-level key set to exactly {hooks} as a parse-safety regression guard.

       Steering / fail-open, NOT an enforcement boundary (docs/ai-environment-
       boundary.md): a missing body (exit 127), any non-2 non-zero exit, bad JSON,
       or a timeout all let the tool call continue (only exit 2 blocks), so
       rendering this before agent-tools has synced is a safe no-op.

       Codex-specific inert stage (honest-label): even a registered+present hook
       is silently skipped until a one-time interactive `/hooks` trust in Codex
       (trust recorded in ~/.codex/config.toml [hooks.state]). Registration alone
       does not activate it.

       Gating (template self-gate, NOT a module `requires`): the file is managed
       for profiles that list codex-settings (personal). The capabilities are
       gated HERE — with BOTH hook capabilities false the file renders EMPTY, and
       chezmoi removes a managed target whose render is empty; with one of them
       false only that capability's events are dropped (agent-hooks-json). A
       `requires:` on the module would instead make .chezmoiignore drop the
       source, which leaves an already-applied ~/.codex/hooks.json lingering
       (chezmoiignore does not prune existing targets) — so disabling the
       capabilities would NOT remove a live hook. Doing it in-template means
       flipping them off truly removes the file on the next apply (Codex review
       #184; verified in test-codex-settings.sh). */ -}}
{{- if or (index $caps "enableGitHubIsolatedReader") (index $caps "enableQualityLoopHooks") -}}
{
{{ template "agent-hooks-json" (dict "caps" $caps "homeDir" .chezmoi.homeDir "agentDir" ".codex" "timeout" 10) }}
}
{{ end -}}
